 # Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling
 
-Also called: FD method, Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Shear-Warp Factorization, Frequency domain based method, Resampling in the Frequency Domain
+Also called: FD method, Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Shear-Warp Factorization, Frequency domain based method, High-Quality Resampling in Frequency Domain, Resampling in the Frequency Domain
 
 ## Overview
-Frequency Domain Volume Rendering with Sinc Resampling is a direct volume ray casting technique that performs resampling (zooming and shearing) in the frequency domain using Fourier transforms to avoid blurring artifacts associated with spatial domain interpolation, particularly for large zoom factors [S1]. It exploits signal processing ideas such as the shifting theorem, the packing theorem, and the derivative theorem to achieve the best theoretical quality by using the ideal interpolation filter (sinc) [S1]. Unlike standard spatial domain filters (e.g., B-spline, Catmull-Rom) or methods that zoom the intermediate image, this method resamples every volume slice in object space before projection, ensuring perfect reconstruction if the sampling meets Nyquist criteria [S1].
+Frequency Domain Volume Rendering (FD method) is a volume rendering algorithm that performs resampling using the ideal interpolation filter (sinc) to achieve the best theoretical quality, exploiting signal processing ideas such as the shifting theorem, the packing theorem, and the derivative theorem [S1]. It solves the problem of blurring artifacts associated with spatial domain interpolation, particularly for large zoom factors, by performing resampling (zooming and shearing) in the frequency domain using Fourier transforms [S1][S2, unofficial]. Unlike [Trilinear Interpolation](1531-trilinear-interpolation.md) or [Fast tri-cubic interpolation](848-fast-tri-cubic-interpolation.md) which operate in the spatial domain, this method allows perfect reconstruction of the original signal if the sampling was done according to the Nyquist criteria [S1].
 
 ## How it works
-The method integrates frequency-domain resampling into the Shear-Warp factorization pipeline [S1]. It transforms the volume to the frequency domain, applies shifts and zooms via zero-padding and phase multiplication, and transforms back to the spatial domain for compositing and warping [S1].
+The method integrates frequency-domain resampling into the Shear-Warp factorization pipeline. It transforms the volume to the frequency domain, applies shifts and scaling via zero-padding and phase multiplication, transforms back to the spatial domain for compositing, and finally warps the intermediate image.
 
 ### Inputs and Outputs
-*   **Input:** 3D volume dataset $V(i,j,k)$ with dimensions $I \times J \times K$ [G].
-*   **Output:** 2D rendered image buffer of size $N \times N$ [S1].
+*   **Input:** A 3D volume dataset $V(i,j,k)$ with dimensions $I \times J \times K$.
+*   **Output:** A 2D rendered image.
+*   **Parameters:**
+    *   $s_0$: Desired sampling distance in the k-direction (e.g., 0.05 for Stanford Bunny) [S1].
+    *   $z$: Scene scale factor [S1].
+    *   $M$: Maximal volume extension ($\max(I, J, K)$) [S1].
+    *   $N$: Neighborhood extend for Lanczos windowed-sinc (if used for warping) [S2, unofficial].
 
 ### Data Structures
-*   **Volume Data:** Real-valued array $V$ [G].
-*   **Frequency Domain Data:** Complex-valued array $\hat{V}$ [G]. Due to the Hermitian symmetry of real functions, memory can be optimized to store only half the spectrum [S1].
-*   **Intermediate Image:** 2D array storing color and transparency (density) values [G].
-
